{"seq":4,"slug":"washington-political-principles","title":"乔治·华盛顿：政治理念与原则","stage":"DONE","category":"george-washington","categoryLabel":"乔治·华盛顿","chapterCount":3,"docCount":8,"url":"/l-doc-books/c/leaders/george-washington/washington-political-principles","rating":{"composite":3.66,"verdict":"stable","groups":{"quality":3.4,"coverage":2.5,"positioning":null,"strategic":5},"weights":{"quality":0.3,"coverage":0.25,"positioning":0.2,"strategic":0.25},"profile":null},"hubDir":"/Users/wangyang.learnwy/learnwy/learnwy/l-doc-hub/hubs/leaders/08_george-washington/4_washington-political-principles","introHtml":"<h1 id=\"乔治-华盛顿-政治理念与原则\">乔治·华盛顿：政治理念与原则</h1><p>乔治·华盛顿（George Washington, 1732–1799）常被冠以&quot;建国之父&quot;之名，但人们记住的往往是他骑马跨过特拉华河的形象，而非他留下的政治思想。事实上，华盛顿并非系统的政治理论家，他没有像麦迪逊或汉密尔顿那样写下《联邦党人文集》，也没有像杰斐逊那样起草过宣言式的纲领文本。他的政治理念散落在书信、阁议记录、就职演说与最著名的1796年《告别辞》之中，更体现在他面对权力时一次次具体的选择里。</p>\n<p>本知识库试图把这些零散的言行还原为一套可以辨认的政治原则。它的核心命题是：在一个尚无先例的共和国里，最重要的不是权力如何被夺取，而是权力如何被自我约束。1783年12月23日，华盛顿在安纳波利斯向邦联国会交还军权；1797年，他在两届任期后主动退位。这两次&quot;放弃权力&quot;，比他赢得的任何一场战役都更深刻地塑造了美国的政治形态。</p>\n<p>华盛顿的思想有几条清晰的主线。其一是共和德性——他相信共和制只能建立在人民的道德与公共精神之上，制度本身无法替代品格。其二是联邦统一——他把&quot;合众为一&quot;视为一切自由的前提，反复警告地域分裂的危险。其三是对党争与派系的深刻警惕，尽管他本人在执政后期也不可避免地被卷入联邦党与共和党的对立。其四是外交上的独立自主，集中体现于1793年的《中立宣言》与《告别辞》中&quot;避免长期同盟&quot;的告诫。</p>\n<p>然而，任何严肃的传记都无法回避华盛顿身上的根本矛盾：这位高举自由旗帜的人，终其一生是弗吉尼亚最大的奴隶主之一。他在遗嘱中安排在妻子去世后解放自己名下的奴隶，是建国一代弗吉尼亚籍总统中唯一这样做的人，但这迟来的姿态既不能抹去制度之恶，也不足以使他成为废奴者。</p>\n<p>本库以中立、求证的方式处理这些主题。我们引用具体的日期、地点、文献与当事人之言，既呈现华盛顿被后世推崇的政治智慧，也不回避其历史局限。读者将看到一个在&quot;已知的革命&quot;与&quot;未竟的理想&quot;之间不断权衡的真实政治人物，而非神龛上的偶像。</p>\n<p>全库分为三章：第一章探讨联邦统一与共和德性的根基；第二章剖析他对党争、权力与宪政自制的思考；第三章考察其外交独立原则与围绕奴隶制的道德遗产。三章之间互为印证，共同构成对华盛顿政治理念的完整勾勒。</p>\n","chapters":[{"seq":1,"slug":"union-and-republican-virtue","title":"联邦统一与共和德性","docCount":3,"url":"/l-doc-books/c/leaders/george-washington/washington-political-principles/union-and-republican-virtue","docs":[{"seq":1,"slug":"republican-virtue-and-public-morality","title":"共和德性与公共道德","url":"/l-doc-books/c/leaders/george-washington/washington-political-principles/union-and-republican-virtue/republican-virtue-and-public-morality"},{"seq":2,"slug":"the-union-as-supreme-good","title":"联邦统一作为至高之善","url":"/l-doc-books/c/leaders/george-washington/washington-political-principles/union-and-republican-virtue/the-union-as-supreme-good"},{"seq":3,"slug":"religious-liberty-and-civic-pluralism","title":"宗教自由与公民多元","url":"/l-doc-books/c/leaders/george-washington/washington-political-principles/union-and-republican-virtue/religious-liberty-and-civic-pluralism"}]},{"seq":2,"slug":"parties-power-and-constitutional-restraint","title":"党争、权力与宪政自制","docCount":3,"url":"/l-doc-books/c/leaders/george-washington/washington-political-principles/parties-power-and-constitutional-restraint","docs":[{"seq":1,"slug":"warnings-against-faction-and-party","title":"对党争与派系的警惕","url":"/l-doc-books/c/leaders/george-washington/washington-political-principles/parties-power-and-constitutional-restraint/warnings-against-faction-and-party"},{"seq":2,"slug":"voluntary-surrender-of-power","title":"主动放弃权力","url":"/l-doc-books/c/leaders/george-washington/washington-political-principles/parties-power-and-constitutional-restraint/voluntary-surrender-of-power"},{"seq":3,"slug":"executive-precedent-and-rule-of-law","title":"行政先例与法治","url":"/l-doc-books/c/leaders/george-washington/washington-political-principles/parties-power-and-constitutional-restraint/executive-precedent-and-rule-of-law"}]},{"seq":3,"slug":"foreign-policy-and-moral-legacy","title":"外交独立与道德遗产","docCount":2,"url":"/l-doc-books/c/leaders/george-washington/washington-political-principles/foreign-policy-and-moral-legacy","docs":[{"seq":1,"slug":"neutrality-and-foreign-entanglements","title":"中立原则与避免外国纠缠","url":"/l-doc-books/c/leaders/george-washington/washington-political-principles/foreign-policy-and-moral-legacy/neutrality-and-foreign-entanglements"},{"seq":2,"slug":"slavery-and-the-unfinished-ideal","title":"奴隶制与未竟的理想","url":"/l-doc-books/c/leaders/george-washington/washington-political-principles/foreign-policy-and-moral-legacy/slavery-and-the-unfinished-ideal"}]}]}